<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-map-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Map convergence
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine whether or not the given real-valued function converges at the given input. If the function converges, find the limit and express your answer using limit notation.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> f \colon \R \to \R </m> defined piecewise by the formula
        
        <md>
          f \of{ x } =
            \begin{cases}
              -1 \amp x \lt 0 \\
              0 \amp x = 0 \\
              1 \amp x \gt 0
            \end{cases}
        </md>.
        
        Determine whether or not <m> f </m> converges at <m> 0 </m>. If <m> f </m> converges at <m> 0 </m>, find the limit and express your answer using limit notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> f </m> does not converge at <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ f \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ n } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ 1 } = 1
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ f \of{ -\frac{ 1 }{ n } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ -1 } = -1
        </md>.
        
        If <m> f </m> converged at <m> 0 </m>, then the above limits to have the same value. Since they do not have the same value, <m> f </m> does not converge at <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> g \colon \R \to \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          g \of{ y } = y^{ 2 } - 2 y + 1
        </md>.
        
        Determine whether or not <m> g </m> converges at <m> 0 </m>. If <m> g </m> converges at <m> 0 </m>, find the limit and express your answer using limit notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> g </m> converges at <m> 0 </m> to <m> 1 </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ y \to 0 }{ g \of{ y } } = 1
        </md>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number, and let <m> \delta = \min \of{ \set{ 1 , \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } } } \gt 0 </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ g \of{ y } , 1 } \amp = \abs{ y^{ 2 } - 2 y + 1 - 1 } = \abs{ y^{ 2 } - 2 y } = \abs{ y } \cdot \abs{ y - 2 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \abs{ y - 0 } \cdot 3 = 3 d \of{ y , 0 } \lt 3 \delta \leq 3 \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all <m> y \in \R </m> so that <m> 0 \lt d \of{ y , 0 } \lt \delta </m>, and so <m> g </m> converges to <m> 1 </m> at <m> 0 </m>. Since <m> 0 </m> is a limit point of <m> \R </m>, this limit is unique; that is,
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ y \to 0 }{ g \of{ y } } = 1
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> h \colon \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } \to \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          h \of{ z } = \sqrt{ z }
        </md>.
        
        Determine whether or not <m> h </m> converges at <m> 4 </m>. If <m> h </m> converges at <m> 4 </m>, find the limit and express your answer using limit notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        Use the fact that <m> \abs{ \sqrt{ x } - 2 } \cdot \abs{ \sqrt{ x } + 2 } = \abs{ x - 4 } </m> for all non-negative real numbers <m> x \geq 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> \lim_{ x \to 4 }{ h \of{ z } } = 2 </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number, and let <m> \delta = \min \of{ \set{ 3 , 3 \epsilon } } \gt 0 </m>. We observe that if <m> d \of{ z , 4 } \lt 3 </m>, then <m> \abs{ z - 4 } \lt 3 </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            z \amp \geq 1
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \sqrt{ z } \amp \geq 1
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \sqrt{ z } + 2 \amp \geq 3
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \frac{ 1 }{ \sqrt{ z } + 2 } \amp \leq \frac{ 1 }{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We conclude that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ h \of{ z } , 2 } \amp = \abs{ \sqrt{ z } - 2 } = \frac{ \abs{ z - 4 } }{ \sqrt{ z } + 2 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \lt \frac{ d \of{ z , 4 } }{ 3 } \lt \frac{ \delta }{ 3 } \leq \frac{ 3 \epsilon }{ 3 } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all <m> z \in \R </m> so that <m> 0 \lt d \of{ z , 4 } \lt \delta </m>, and so <m> h </m> converges to <m> 2 </m> at <m> 4 </m>. Since <m> 4 </m> is a limit point of <m> \R </m>, this limit is unique; that is,
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ z \to 4 }{ h \of{ z } } = 2
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
